glish cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"]]},{"h":"More from English cuisine","links":[["Angels on horseback","dish/angels-on-horseback"],["Clotted cream","dish/clotted-cream"],["Crème anglaise","dish/creme-anglaise"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Macaroni and cheese","dish/macaroni-and-cheese"],["Maids of honour tart","dish/maids-of-honour-tart"],["Manchester tart","dish/manchester-tart"],["Mock turtle soup","dish/mock-turtle-soup"],["Pie and mash","dish/pie-and-mash"],["Pork pie","dish/pork-pie"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"],["Windsor soup","dish/windsor-soup"]]}]},"ingredient/melon":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/melon","n":"Melon","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A melon is any of various plants of the family Cucurbitaceae with sweet, edible, and fleshy fruit. It can also specifically refer to Cucumis melo, commonly known as the \"true melon\" or simply \"melon\". The term \"melon\" can apply to both the plant and its fruit. Botanically, a melon is a kind of berry, specifically a \"pepo\". The word melon derives from Latin melopepo, which is the latinization of the Greek μηλοπέπων (mēlopepōn), meaning \"melon\", itself a compound of μῆλον (mēlon), \"apple\", treefruit (of any kind)\" and πέπων (pepōn), amongst others \"a kind of gourd or melon\". Many different cultivars have been produced, particularly of the true melon, such as the cantaloupe and honeydew."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-melon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Calisson","dish/calisson"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Calisson","dish/calisson"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Almond meal","ingredient/almond-meal"],["Candied fruit","ingredient/candied-fruit"],["Fondant icing","ingredient/fondant-icing"],["Syrup","ingredient/syrup"]]}]},"dish/christmas-pudding":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/christmas-pudding","n":"Christmas pudding","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Christmas pudding is a sweet boiled or steamed pudding traditionally served as part of Christmas dinner in Britain. It has its origins in medieval England, with early recipes making use of dried fruit, suet, breadcrumbs, flour, eggs and spice, along with liquid such as milk or fortified wine. Later, recipes became more elaborate. In 1845, cookery writer Eliza Acton wrote the first recipe for a dish called \"Christmas pudding\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-christmas-pudding"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_pudding"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Treacle","ingredient/treacle"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","ref":"site/greatbritishchefs-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["68–86 Bar and Restaurant","dish/68-86-bar-and-restaurant"],["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dinner by Heston Blumenthal","dish/dinner-by-heston-blumenthal"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Gidleigh Park","dish/gidleigh-park"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Restaurant Andrew Fairlie","dish/restaurant-andrew-fairlie"],["Restaurant James Sommerin","dish/restaurant-james-sommerin"],["Rhodes Twenty Four","dish/rhodes-twenty-four"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Rose & Crown Pub & Dining Room","dish/rose-crown-pub-dining-room"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"]]}]},"dish/city-tavern":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/city-tavern","n":"City Tavern","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Thirteen Colonies cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["City Tavern is a late-20th century replica of a historic 18th-century tavern and hotel which once stood on the site. The original tavern was frequented by the Founding Fathers of the United States and other colonial leaders. The original building partially burned in 1834; the remains were demolished in 1854."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-city-tavern"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Tavern"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Thirteen Colonies cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-thirteen-colonies"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Colonial Williamsburg: Recipes","ref":"site/colonialwilliamsburg-org","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"colonialwilliamsburg.org","note":"Historic foodways recipes from the Colonial Williamsburg living history museum, adapted from 18th-century sources.","url":"https://www.colonialwilliamsburg.org/learn/recipes/","suggested":true}]}]},"ingredient/oatmeal":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/oatmeal","n":"Oatmeal","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Oatmeal is a preparation of oats that have been dehusked, steamed, and flattened, or a coarse flour of hulled oat grains (groats) that have either been milled (ground), rolled, or steel-cut. Ground oats are also called white oats. Steel-cut oats are known as coarse oatmeal, Irish oatmeal, or pinhead oats. Rolled oats were traditionally thick old-fashioned oats, but they can be made thinner or smaller and may be categorized as quick oatmeal or instant oatmeal depending on the cooking time required, which is determined by the size of the oats and the amount of precooking."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-oatmeal"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oatmeal"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Haggis","dish/haggis"],["Haggis pakora","dish/haggis-pakora"],["Stornoway black pudding","dish/stornoway-black-pudding"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Oatmeal ball","dish/oatmeal-ball"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Oats","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":22975839,"top":[["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",3747559],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",3740299],["Poland"